---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import FurtherReading from '../../components/FurtherReading.astro';
import SeaBreak from '../../components/SeaBreak.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import CTABand from '../../components/CTABand.astro';
import Layers from '../../components/Layers.astro';
import { celestial, ocean } from '../../config/images';
const crumbs = [
  { label: 'Explore Astrology', href: '/explore/' },
  { label: 'House Systems', href: '/explore/house-systems/' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="House Systems: why astrologers divide the chart differently"
  description="Whole sign, Placidus, equal house, Regiomontanus, and other house systems: what changes, what stays the same, and why serious astrologers can use different methods."
  path="/explore/house-systems/"
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
>
  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={crumbs} />
  <section class="hero hero--split">
    <div class="container">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Explore Astrology</p>
        <h1>House systems</h1>
        <p class="lede">This is one of the places where astrology refuses to become tidy. Different house systems can place the same planet in different houses, and serious astrologers can still disagree about which framework is most useful.</p>
      </div>
      <img class="hero__img" src={ocean.coralReefBlueHour.src} srcset={ocean.coralReefBlueHour.srcset} sizes="100vw" alt={ocean.coralReefBlueHour.alt} width={ocean.coralReefBlueHour.width} height={ocean.coralReefBlueHour.height} fetchpriority="high" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container prose flow">
      <h2>The problem being solved</h2>
      <p>
        The zodiac is a 360° circle divided into twelve signs. Houses are also twelve divisions, but
        they are tied to a local horizon and meridian. Different systems solve the problem of
        dividing that local space in different ways. The result is not one chart with different
        planets; it is the same planetary positions organized through different house frameworks.
      </p>

      <h2>The systems you will meet most often</h2>
      <h3>Whole sign houses</h3>
      <p>
        In whole sign houses, the entire sign containing the Ascendant becomes the first house, the
        next sign becomes the second, and so on. The method is simple and has deep historical roots.
        The Midheaven remains an important point but does not have to define the tenth-house cusp.
      </p>
      <h3>Placidus</h3>
      <p>
        Placidus is common in modern Western astrology and produces houses of unequal size. It is
        calculated differently from whole sign and can move planets into neighboring houses,
        especially when they are close to a cusp.
      </p>
      <h3>Equal house</h3>
      <p>
        Equal house begins the first house at the Ascendant degree and then divides the chart into
        twelve equal 30° houses. Like whole sign, it does not require the Midheaven to be the
        tenth-house cusp.
      </p>
      <p>
        Other systems in regular use include Koch, Porphyry, Regiomontanus, Campanus, and Alcabitius.
        Horary astrologers may favor systems that differ from those used in natal work. The method
        should fit the technique rather than functioning as a badge of identity.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <SeaBreak image={celestial.keys} />

  <section class="section section--sea">
    <div class="container prose flow">
      <h2>What changes, and what does not</h2>
      <p><strong>House system does not change:</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li>the zodiacal sign and degree of each planet</li>
        <li>the aspects between planets</li>
        <li>the Ascendant and Midheaven degrees themselves</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>House system can change:</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li>which house a planet occupies</li>
        <li>which signs fall on intermediate house cusps</li>
        <li>some rulership chains and house-based interpretive emphasis</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        That is not a trivial difference, but it is also not a completely different chart. I think
        this is a useful example of why astrology benefits from humility: technique matters, and
        technique does not erase judgment.
      </p>

      <Layers kind="mo">
        <p>
          I have studied and used more than one house system. I do not need every chart to prove one
          system universally correct. If a placement changes houses between methods and that change
          matters to the question, I am comfortable looking more closely rather than pretending the
          ambiguity is embarrassing. The chart belongs to the person, not to my need for a tidy rule.
        </p>
      </Layers>

      <Layers kind="differs">
        <p>
          Different traditions and techniques favor different systems, and astrologers often arrive
          at their preferences through training, historical method, and experience with charts. That
          disagreement is real. It is also one reason it is useful to ask an astrologer what method
          they are using rather than assuming every chart is being divided the same way.
        </p>
      </Layers>

      <h2>What to do about it as a client</h2>
      <p>
        You do not need to pick a side before having a reading. Ask which system is being used and,
        if a placement is close to a boundary or another technique is relevant, ask whether the
        astrologer considers more than one framework. The explanation should make sense without
        requiring you to adopt an astrology-school identity of your own.
      </p>
      <p>
        Related: <a href="/explore/angles/">the four angles</a>,
        <a href="/explore/houses/">the twelve houses</a>, and
        <a href="/explore/schools/">schools and traditions</a>.
      </p>
      <FurtherReading />
    </div>
  </section>

  <CTABand heading="The method should serve the chart" body="Different house systems can emphasize different parts of the same chart. The useful question is what helps us understand the person and the question in front of us." buttonLabel="See the readings" buttonHref="/readings/" />
</BaseLayout>
